When performing chest compressions on an infant, first ensure the scene is safe and check the infant's responsiveness. Place the infant on a firm surface, ideally on their back. Using two fingers (usually the index and middle), position them in the center of the infant's chest just below the nipple line. Push down about 1.5 inches deep at a rate of 100-120 compressions per minute. Remember to allow the chest to fully recoil between compressions. 

(Note: This is a simplified guide and in real situations, it's necessary to get professional medical help immediately).
